<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  RuView sensing server, the aggregator for the ESP32-S3 CSI nodes.

  Every argument in ProgramArguments below is load-bearing; none are defaults.
  They are described here WITHOUT their leading hyphens on purpose: a double
  hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment, launchd's lenient parser accepts it
  anyway, and the file then throws in every strict parser that reads it
  (plistlib, and the doctor check that uses it). This file was unparseable for
  exactly that reason until 2026-08-21.

    udp-bind 0.0.0.0   The server binds UDP to 127.0.0.1 by DEFAULT, which
                       silently drops every frame arriving from a LAN node.
                       Without this flag the nodes stream perfectly and the
                       API reports zero nodes, with nothing logged.
    udp-allow          Required to accept a routable bind. Scoped to the LAN
                       rather than the insecure-lan variant: the UDP data plane
                       is NOT authenticated, so anything that can reach the port
                       can inject CSI frames.
    ws-port 3001       The WebSocket path is /ws/sensing, and it is served on
                       BOTH 3000 and 3001. The PATH is the part that matters:
                       a bare ws://127.0.0.1:3001 with no path is refused with a
                       non-101 status and no explanation. Verified 2026-08-21.
    ui-path            Absolute on purpose. The documented value is the relative
                       "../ui", which only resolves if the process starts in
                       v2/, and a working-directory dependency is not something
                       to rely on under launchd.

  Logs: ~/Library/Logs/ruview.log (and .err.log)
  Check it:  curl -s localhost:3000/api/v1/nodes
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.cleetus.ruview</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Users/grayson/RuView/v2/target/release/sensing-server</string>
    <string>--source</string><string>esp32</string>
    <string>--http-port</string><string>3000</string>
    <string>--ws-port</string><string>3001</string>
    <string>--udp-port</string><string>5005</string>
    <string>--udp-bind</string><string>0.0.0.0</string>
    <string>--udp-allow</string><string>192.168.1.0/24</string>
    <string>--ui-path</string><string>/Users/grayson/RuView/ui</string>
    <string>--node-positions</string><string>0.000,0.000,0.75;1.742,2.184,0.75;2.642,0.000,0.75</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>/Users/grayson/RuView/v2</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- Fusion was failing outright: "Timestamp spread 92043 us exceeds guard
         interval 60000 us". The nodes run a 3-slot TDM schedule, so their frames
         are staggered BY DESIGN and the stock 60 ms guard is simply narrower
         than one cycle. guard = slots x slot_us x 1.2, so 3 x 33333 gives a
         ~120 ms guard against the ~92 ms cycle actually measured here. Derived
         from the observed spread, not inflated until the warning stopped. -->
    <key>WDP_TDM_SLOTS</key>
    <string>3</string>
    <key>WDP_TDM_SLOT_US</key>
    <string>33333</string>

    <!-- THE DERIVED GUARD CAN NEVER PASS, and the two lines above are why it
         was set that way. guard = slots x slot_us x 1.2 = 120 ms. But the
         fuser does not compare node CLOCKS at all: multistatic_bridge.rs
         timestamps each frame with the SERVER's arrival time
         (last_time.duration_since(EPOCH)), and node_frames_from_states takes
         simply the LATEST frame held for each node. Under a 3-slot TDM
         schedule a board transmits once per ~100 ms cycle, so "latest frame
         per node" is spread by up to a full cycle before any jitter is added.
         The natural spread therefore sits ABOVE the guard derived from that
         same schedule.

         RAISED 300 -> 500 ms on 2026-08-21, because the fleet's spread drifted
         up and 300 no longer cleared it. Measured live over 90 s with all three
         boards active, sampling the gap between the freshest and stalest node
         (that gap IS what the fuser sees, since it timestamps on arrival):

           p50 68 ms   p90 222 ms   p95 302 ms   p99 488 ms   max 683 ms

         300 ms cleared only 95.0% of healthy cycles; 500 ms clears 99.1% and
         covers p99 with headroom, the same rule that picked 300.

         WHAT IT ACTUALLY BOUGHT, measured as eight 30 s windows at each
         setting, normalised per 1000 frames processed (errors/min alone is
         useless — the frame rate moves):

           300 ms   median 111   worst window 344
           500 ms   median 103   worst window 124

         So the MEDIAN barely moved and the worst-case bursts fell ~3x. Do not
         repeat the first read of this, which compared one 60 s window at each
         setting and appeared to show a 21x improvement; the between-window
         variance is larger than the effect, and a single sample at each end is
         not a measurement. The doctor's fusion check went from pinned at the
         6/min rate-limit ceiling to 4/min, which passes but not comfortably.

         The median stays put because engine_error_count is NOT a pure guard
         counter — it rises from other paths too, so guard rejections can fall
         5x while the counter moves a little.

         AND THE STRUCTURAL POINT: after raising it, the logged exceedances
         re-clustered just above the NEW guard (median 514 ms against 500).
         They did the same at 300. The spread distribution is heavy-tailed, so
         there is always a population sitting just over whatever line is drawn
         and no value of this knob makes the warning disappear. Widening it
         further trades temporal fidelity for a number that will look the
         same.

         DO NOT RAISE IT FURTHER TO CHASE THE LAST 1%. The log's exceedance
         tail runs to 9.9 s and looks like it demands a 10 s guard; it does not.
         That tail is a NODE BEING DOWN — a dropped node's last frame lingers
         until STALE_THRESHOLD (10 s), so those samples are literally that
         constant. Confirmed again 2026-08-21: over 30 h of log, 68% of
         exceedances sit in 300-350 ms (real jitter) and the rest are seconds
         apart (dropouts). A guard wide enough to admit a dropout is fusing
         captures taken seconds apart, which is worse than rejecting them.
         Rejecting a stale frame is CORRECT behaviour.

         The cost is real and worth stating: frames up to 500 ms apart are now
         fused. That is fine for breathing (0.2-0.5 Hz) and presence, and more
         marginal for fast motion than 300 ms was. WDP_GUARD_INTERVAL_US always
         wins over the TDM-derived value (main.rs:7394); the TDM pair is left in
         place because it still sets the soft guard. -->
    <key>WDP_GUARD_INTERVAL_US</key>
    <string>500000</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>/Users/grayson</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- If the binary is mid-rebuild or a port is briefly held, respawning as
       fast as launchd is willing to just burns CPU and floods the log. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>15</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/grayson/Library/Logs/ruview.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/grayson/Library/Logs/ruview.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
